{
  "concept": "An element's position in the periodic table predicts the charge of the ion it forms — group number gives the cation charge for main-group metals and the number of electrons gained for nonmetals — while transition metals break the rule by forming multiple charges.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Title text 'Periodic Table of the Elements' centered at top",
    "Vertical purple 'Period' axis label at far left, rotated",
    "Blue 'Group' label at upper left",
    "Blue group-number headers 1, 2 above the s-block; 3–12 above the d-block (lower, at period-4 height); 13–18 above the p-block",
    "Purple period-number labels 1–7 down the left edge",
    "Period 1 row: blank pale-gray H cell at group 1, He cell at group 18",
    "Group 1 column cells (light purple/lavender fill): Li+, Na+, K+, Rb+, Cs+, Fr+",
    "Group 2 column cells (light green fill): Be2+, Mg2+, Ca2+, Sr2+, Ba2+, Ra2+",
    "d-block grid, groups 3–12 across periods 4–7 (pale tan/cream fill), with labeled cells Cr3+/Cr6+, Mn2+, Fe2+/Fe3+, Co2+, Ni2+, Cu+/Cu2+, Zn2+, Ag+, Cd2+, Pt2+, Au+/Au3+, Hg2^2+/Hg2+; remaining d-block cells empty",
    "p-block cells groups 13–17: Al3+ (white), C4− (white), N3−, O2−, P3−, S2−, As3−, Se2−, Te2− (pink/salmon fill), F−, Cl−, Br−, I−, At− (pale blue-violet fill), plus empty white cells (B, Si, Ge, Sn, Pb, Sb, Bi, Po)",
    "Group 18 noble gas column (cyan fill, no charge labels): He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe, Rn",
    "Red rectangular outline around the group-3 cells of periods 6 and 7 marking the f-block insertion point",
    "Red curved arrow from the red box down-right to the f-block strip",
    "Lanthanide strip: 14 pale-green cells labeled with '*' marker at left, inside a gray-bordered container",
    "Actinide strip: 14 pale-pink cells labeled with '**' marker at left, inside the same gray container",
    "Detail/readout panel (added by demo) showing selected element symbol, name, ion charge, electron configuration, and the group-trend rule",
    "Column highlight overlay rectangle used by the group slider",
    "Legend keyed to the active color scheme"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "selectElement",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Why a specific element carries the charge it does — the electron configuration behind the group-number rule, and why some transition metals list two charges."
    },
    {
      "id": "groupSweep",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Highlight group",
      "range": [1, 18, 1],
      "default": 1,
      "teaches": "That charge is a property of the column: sweeping left to right shows +1, +2, then variable charges in the d-block, then −3, −2, −1, and finally zero for the noble gases."
    },
    {
      "id": "colorScheme",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Color by",
      "options": ["Element type", "Charge value"],
      "default": "Element type",
      "teaches": "That the metal/nonmetal/noble-gas classification and the sign-and-magnitude of ionic charge are two views of the same periodic pattern — cations on the left, anions on the right, inert in the last column."
    },
    {
      "id": "hoverCell",
      "type": "hover",
      "label": "Cell tooltip",
      "teaches": "Quick identification of unlabeled cells (metalloids, f-block, empty d-block) without cluttering the table with text."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The table as drawn",
      "narration": "Each cell shows the ion an element most commonly forms. Notice that whole columns share a charge, and that the noble gases on the far right carry no label at all.",
      "state": { "colorScheme": "Element type", "groupSweep": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Group 1: lose one electron",
      "narration": "Highlighting group 1 shows every alkali metal forming a 1+ ion. Each has a single valence s electron and sheds it to reach a noble-gas configuration.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 1, "colorScheme": "Element type" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Group 2: lose two",
      "narration": "Sliding to group 2, every alkaline earth metal forms 2+. For main-group metals the cation charge simply equals the group number.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 2, "colorScheme": "Element type" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The d-block breaks the rule",
      "narration": "Sweep into groups 3–12 and the pattern fails: iron shows both 2+ and 3+, copper 1+ and 2+, chromium 3+ and 6+. Transition metals can remove d electrons as well as s electrons, so group number no longer predicts a single charge.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 8, "colorScheme": "Element type" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Nonmetals gain electrons",
      "narration": "At group 16 the labels turn negative — O2−, S2−, Se2−, Te2−. These elements are two columns short of a noble gas, so they gain two electrons instead of losing any.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 16, "colorScheme": "Element type" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Halogens and the noble-gas target",
      "narration": "Group 17 needs just one electron, giving 1− ions. Group 18 already has a full valence shell, which is why those cells are blank.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 17, "colorScheme": "Element type" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Recolor by charge",
      "narration": "Switching the color scheme to charge value makes the symmetry obvious: a block of positive charges on the left, a block of negative charges on the right, variable charges in the middle, and a neutral column at the edge.",
      "state": { "colorScheme": "Charge value", "groupSweep": 18 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Ionic charge is not memorized element by element — it is read off the column: main-group elements gain or lose however many electrons it takes to reach the nearest noble gas, and the d-block is the region where that shortcut stops working.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate the standard 18-column, 7-period grid of rounded square cells with thin gray borders and a small gap between cells, with the f-block detached below as two 14-cell strips inside a gray-bordered container. Keep the published fills: group 1 lavender, group 2 pale green, d-block pale tan/cream, groups 15–16 nonmetals salmon-pink, group 17 pale blue-violet, group 18 cyan, unlabeled p-block cells white, hydrogen cell pale gray. Labels are bold black element symbols with superscript charges; cells with two common charges stack the two labels (Cr3+ over Cr6+, Fe2+ over Fe3+, Cu+ over Cu2+, Au+ over Au3+, Hg2^2+ over Hg2+). Group numbers are bold blue above their columns — 1 and 2 at the top, 3–12 dropped down to sit just above period 4, 13–18 at the top right. Period numbers are bold purple at the left, with a rotated purple 'Period' label. Keep the red rectangle outlining the group-3 cells of periods 6 and 7 and the red curved arrow sweeping down-right to the lanthanide/actinide strips, with '*' and '**' markers at the strips' left edges. Add an unobtrusive detail panel in the empty upper-left region (the gap between group 2 and group 13 in periods 1–3) for the click readout. Column highlight should dim non-selected cells rather than recolor them; the charge-value color scheme should map to a diverging scale — warm reds for positive charges scaled by magnitude, cool blues for negative, gray for variable d-block, and white/neutral for the noble gases."
}

